#import "../template.typ": *

#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[On the mat] <on-the-mat>

#ix("Quick reference")
The book on one page, for between attempts. @reading-method has the full method.

// two columns of about the same height, so the card fits on one page
#columns(2, gutter: 1.5em)[
#remember[Before you pull on][
  + Find the start, finish and way down.
  + Stand back, walk round, read the top.
  + Work out which way each hold faces.
  + Count the hands: L or R for each hold.
  + Find the feet for the start and the crux.
  + Find the crux, the traps and the rests.
  + Ask what movement the setter is testing.
  + Mime the crux once.
  + Climb, then compare it with your plan.
]

#remember[When it doesn't go][
  + Name why you fell: a misjudged hold, a lost sequence or a surprise.
  + Ask the three questions again. Look the symptom up in #link(<troubleshooting>)[the troubleshooting table].
  + After four to six attempts without progress, change something: rest longer, change the sequence or stop for the day.
]

#colbreak()

#remember[Three questions for every move][
  + *Where is my weight?* Can I get my hips over a foot, or closer to a steep wall?
  + *Which way do these holds pull?* Where must my body be for each hold to work?
  + *What stops me rotating?* If I let go with one hand, what holds me in?
]

#remember[One thing from each chapter][
  + *Feet.* Let your legs do the work. Choose the foothold that puts the next hold just within reach.
  + *Body.* Keep your weight on your feet, and put your body where each hold works. Stop the swing before you let go.
  + *Hands.* Pull each hold opposite to the way it faces. A straight arm points along the pull.
  + *Dynamic.* Throw with your legs and hips, not your arms. The hand arrives while you're still moving.
  + *Terrain.* Read the wall angle first. On a slab, stand over your feet. On an overhang, pull in with your toes.
]
]
